`;function rf(){return(0,na.jsxs)(Xd,{id:"hero",children:[(0,na.jsx)(Jd,{initial:{y:-10,opacity:0},animate:{y:0,opacity:1},transition:{duration:.5},children:"ABOUT"}),(0,na.jsxs)(Zd,{initial:{y:-12,opacity:0},whileInView:{y:0,opacity:1},transition:{duration:.5,ease:"easeOut"},children:[(0,na.jsx)(ef,{children:"Full\u2011stack software engineer (Tech Lead) with experience building large\u2011scale reporting systems for NYPD Officers. I work across C#/.NET, Angular, TypeScript, and SQL Server, with interests in finance, statistics, and machine learning. This site highlights work beyond a resume \u2014 academic ML/Stats, finance analytics, system design diagrams, OOD exercises, and data structures and algorithms practices."}),(0,na.jsxs)(tf,{children:[(0,na.jsx)("a",{href:"mailto:ying.wen.ok@gmail.com",children:"Email"}),(0,na.jsx)("a",{href:"https://www.linkedin.com/in/ying-wen",target:"_blank",rel:"noreferrer",children:"LinkedIn"}),(0,na.jsx)("a",{href:"https://github.com/ying-wen-ok",target:"_blank",rel:"noreferrer",children:"GitHub"}),(0,na.jsx)("a",{href:"https://leetcode.com/u/YingLCAccount/",target:"_blank",rel:"noreferrer",children:"LeetCode"})]})]}),(0,na.jsx)(nf,{src:Gd,alt:"Me",viewport:{once:!1,amount:.6}})]})}const af=[{slug:"ModelValidationFamaFrench3FactorsModel",title:"Model Validation \u2013 Fama-French 3 Factors Model",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/ModelValidationFamaFrench3FactorsModel/cover.png",hero:"/images/ModelValidationFamaFrench3FactorsModel/cover.png",pdfFile:"/Model Validation \u2013 Fama-French 3 Factors Model.pdf",tags:["Finance","Statistics"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Finance \xb7 Statistics \xb7 FamaFrench 3 Factors Model \xb7 Model Validation",duration:"01/2020 - 05/2020",tools:"",links:[]},desc:["Background","The Fama-French 3 Factors Model is a widely used asset pricing model that expands on the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) by including size and value risk factors in addition to the market factor. This project validates the model using regression analysis on selected technology stocks and ETFs, and tests its robustness under special market conditions and noisy data scenarios.","Objective"," \xb7 Estimate factor loadings (betas) for each stock/ETF using historical return data."," \xb7 Evaluate the model's explanatory power and statistical significance for each asset."," \xb7 Assess the model\u2019s robustness during the COVID-19 pandemic period."," \xb7 Test model stability by adding noise to the data and observing changes in factor loadings and fit.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data: Monthly returns for GOOG, FB, AMZN, TSLA, SPYG, and SPHD; Fama-French 3 factors data from Kenneth R. French\u2019s online library."," \xb7 Regression Analysis: Multiple linear regression with excess returns as dependent variable and the three Fama-French factors as independent variables."," \xb7 Special Period Test: Subset analysis for the COVID-19 pandemic period to observe changes in factor loadings and model fit."," \xb7 Noise Robustness Test: Added controlled random noise to return data to evaluate parameter stability."," \xb7 Tools: Excel with statistical analysis functions.","Results"," \xb7 For all assets, the market factor was highly significant, with varying significance for SMB (Small Minus Big) and HML (High Minus Low) factors."," \xb7 Growth-oriented assets like GOOG, FB, and AMZN showed negative loadings on HML, consistent with growth characteristics."," \xb7 The model\u2019s explanatory power (R\xb2) ranged from moderate to high across assets, with TSLA showing more idiosyncratic variation."," \xb7 Pandemic-period analysis revealed changes in factor sensitivities, especially higher market beta for certain tech stocks."," \xb7 Noise-added tests indicated that while coefficients varied slightly, the general factor relationships remained stable.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 The Fama-French 3 Factors Model captures significant risk exposures for both tech stocks and ETFs, with clear differences between growth and value profiles."," \xb7 Market shocks like the COVID-19 pandemic can alter factor loadings, highlighting the importance of time-specific validation."," \xb7 Robustness checks with noise confirm the stability of the model\u2019s conclusions under small data perturbations."],gallery:[]},{slug:"QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis",title:"Quadratic Discriminant Analysis (QDA) vs. Optimal Bayes Classifier (BC)",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/QDA.png",hero:"/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/QDA.png",pdfFile:"",tags:["Machine Learning","Statistics","R","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Machine Learning \xb7 Data Mining \xb7 Statistical Learning \xb7 Optimal Bayes Classifer \xb7 Bayes Classifer ",duration:"09/2019 - 01/2020",tools:"R",links:[]},desc:["Background","Quadratic Discriminant Analysis (QDA) is a statistical classification method that models each class with its own covariance matrix, allowing for flexible decision boundaries. The Optimal Bayes Classifier (Optimal BC) represents the theoretical ideal, making predictions based on the true underlying probability distributions.","Objective","The goal of this project was to compare the performance of QDA and Optimal BC across different sample sizes, and to examine how sample size affects their relative classification accuracy.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data Setup: Two separate experiments were run with sample sizes of n = 100 and n = 1000."," \xb7 Evaluation: Classification performance was analyzed across all label pairs, with special attention to the separation between label 1 and label 3, where classification difficulty is higher."," \xb7 Approach: Both QDA and Optimal BC models were trained and tested under identical conditions, ensuring a fair comparison.","Results"," \xb7 With n = 100, both classifiers performed well overall, but Optimal BC showed a slight advantage in correctly classifying between label 1 and label 3."," \xb7 With n = 1000, the performance difference remained similar \u2014 the increase in sample size did not notably alter the gap, and the Optimal BC consistently outperformed QDA in this classification challenge.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 Optimal BC can offer measurable improvements in classification for certain label pairs, even when QDA performs strongly overall."," \xb7 Increasing sample size improves absolute accuracy for both models, but does not necessarily close the performance gap between them."," \xb7 For problems where decision boundary flexibility is crucial, the Optimal BC may yield a more reliable separation."],gallery:["/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/1.png","/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/2.png","/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/3.png","/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/4.png","/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/5.png","/images/QuadraticDiscriminantAnalysis/6.png"]},{slug:"ETFDataAnalysis",title:"Exchange-Traded Funds Data Analysis",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/ETFDataAnalysis/cover.png",hero:"/images/ETFDataAnalysis/cover.png",pdfFile:"",tags:["Machine Learning","Statistics","Python","Finance","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Machine Learning \xb7 Data Mining \xb7 Statistical Learning  \xb7 random forrest  \xb7 radial svm  \xb7 logistic \xb7 logistic lasso \xb7 logistic ridge",duration:"09/2019 - 01/2020",tools:"Python",links:[]},desc:["Background","This project analyzes Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) to identify factors that make an ETF profitable. ETFs are generally considered an easy, low-cost, and relatively low-risk investment vehicle. The dataset, sourced from Kaggle, contains a wide range of ETF attributes, and the goal was to build predictive models to determine whether an ETF will be profitable in the next year.","Objective"," \xb7 Create a binary target variable 'Is profitable' based on the one-year fund return ('fund_return_1year')."," \xb7 Apply and compare multiple machine learning models to predict ETF profitability."," \xb7 Identify the most important features driving ETF performance."," \xb7 Evaluate trade-offs between prediction accuracy and computational efficiency.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data Preparation: Removed columns with more than 10% missing values, dropped rows with missing data, and eliminated identical columns (e.g., fund_name, fund_extended_name)."," \xb7 Final Dataset: 1,811 instances, 30 features, balanced target variable."," \xb7 Models Compared: Ridge Regression, Lasso Regression, Support Vector Machine (SVM), Least Squares (LS), Random Forest (RF)."," \xb7 Hyperparameter Tuning: Compared performance with n-learn values of 0.5 and 0.9."," \xb7 Evaluation Metrics: Error rate, computation time, feature importance."," \xb7 Tools: Python for data cleaning, model training, and evaluation.","Results"," \xb7 Best Efficiency: LS model achieved the lowest error rate (0.09\u20130.10) with high efficiency."," \xb7 Best Predictive Performance: RF model achieved the lowest overall error rate (0.08) but required the longest training time (6.29\u201310.88 seconds)."," \xb7 SVM performance improved slightly with higher n-learn but at a significant time cost."," \xb7 Feature Selection identified top features: fund_yield, portfolio_stocks, price_earnings, category_return_1month, net_annual_expense_ratio_fund, net_annual_expense_ratio_category, price_cashflow, rating_aaa.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 LS is the best choice for balancing prediction performance and computational efficiency."," \xb7 RF delivers the strongest predictive accuracy but at a significant computational cost."," \xb7 Certain financial ratios and expense metrics are strong indicators of ETF profitability."," \xb7 Model selection should consider both accuracy and time cost depending on the application context."],gallery:["/images/ETFDataAnalysis/1.png","/images/ETFDataAnalysis/2.png","/images/ETFDataAnalysis/3.png","/images/ETFDataAnalysis/4.png","/images/ETFDataAnalysis/5.png","/images/ETFDataAnalysis/6.png"]},{slug:"TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation",title:"Trading Strategy Backtesting & Statistical Validation",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/2.png",hero:"/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/2.png",pdfFile:"",tags:["Finance","Statistics","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Finance \xb7 Statistics \xb7 Backtesting",duration:"01/2020 - 05/2020",tools:"EXCEL",links:[]},desc:["Background","Backtesting is a fundamental process in quantitative finance used to evaluate the performance of a trading strategy on historical data before applying it to live markets. It helps assess the profitability, risk profile, and robustness of a strategy.","Objective"," \xb7 Implement a rule-based trading strategy and simulate its performance using historical asset price data."," \xb7 Measure profitability and risk metrics to assess the strategy\u2019s viability."," \xb7 Apply statistical testing to determine if the observed performance is statistically significant.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data Preparation: Collected and cleaned historical price data, ensuring accuracy and completeness."," \xb7 Strategy Implementation: Generated buy/sell signals based on predefined trading rules."," \xb7 Backtesting: Calculated returns, cumulative returns, Value-at-Risk (VaR) at 95% and 99% confidence levels, and exceptions on VaR breaches."," \xb7 Statistical Validation: Applied a binomial test to evaluate whether the number of VaR exceptions falls within the expected non-rejection region."," \xb7 Tools: Microsoft Excel (with VBA macros) for data processing, performance calculation, and statistical testing.","Results"," \xb7 The backtest produced a profitable strategy with controlled risk exposure, as shown in the summary table."," \xb7 VaR exceptions were within the acceptable range for both 95% and 99% confidence levels."," \xb7 The binomial test confirmed that the observed exceptions were consistent with model expectations, indicating robustness."," \xb7 Performance metrics and statistical validation jointly supported the strategy\u2019s effectiveness.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 Backtesting not only measures past performance but also highlights a strategy\u2019s risk characteristics."," \xb7 Statistical tests such as the binomial test are essential for validating whether performance is due to skill or chance."," \xb7 Combining financial metrics with statistical rigor provides a stronger foundation for real-world strategy deployment."],gallery:["/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/1.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/2.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/3.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/4.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/5.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/6.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/7.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/8.png","/images/TradingStrategyBacktestingStatisticalValidation/9.png"]},{slug:"GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis",title:"Glucose Tolerance Test Data Analysis",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/STA9705.png",hero:"/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/STA9705.png",tags:["Statistics","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Multivariate Hypothesis Testing \xb7 Discriminant and Classification Analysis  \xb7 Principal Component Analysis \xb7 Factor Analysis",duration:"09/2019 - 01/2020",tools:"SAS",links:[]},desc:["Background","This project investigates the physiological response of obese individuals compared to a control group during a glucose tolerance test, measured via plasma inorganic phosphate concentration at 8 different time points. The study applied multiple multivariate statistical methods to uncover group differences and identify key measurements contributing to separation.","Objective","The goal was to determine whether there is a statistically significant difference between obese and control groups, identify the time points most relevant for classification, and reduce dimensionality for further analysis.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data: 13 control subjects and 20 obese subjects, each measured at 8 time points (0 to 5 hours after glucose intake)."," \xb7 Statistical Methods: Multivariate hypothesis testing (Hotelling\u2019s T\xb2), discriminant and classification analysis (linear and quadratic classification, k-NN), principal component analysis (PCA), and factor analysis."," \xb7 Software: SAS was used for all calculations, visualizations, and statistical tests.","Results"," \xb7 Multivariate hypothesis testing showed a significant difference between control and obese groups (T\xb2 = 61.999963, p < 0.05)."," \xb7 Discriminant analysis revealed that measurements at 0h (Y1), 0.5h (Y2), and 1.5h (Y4) contributed most to group separation."," \xb7 Quadratic classification achieved 0% resubstitution error; cross-validation error rates varied by method, with k-NN (k=5) achieving 10.19% total error."," \xb7 PCA indicated that the first 2 principal components explained ~82% of variance."," \xb7 Factor analysis grouped the 8 variables into 2\u20133 underlying factors, with the first four time points loading strongly on the first factor.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 Obese individuals exhibit distinct glucose tolerance test response profiles compared to controls, with key differences at specific early time points."," \xb7 Combining discriminant analysis with PCA and factor analysis provides a robust approach to both classification and dimensionality reduction."," \xb7 Early time-point measurements (0h, 0.5h, 1.5h) are the most informative for differentiating between the two groups."],gallery:["/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/1.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/2.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/3.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/4.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/5.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/6.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/7.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/8.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/9.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/10.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/11.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/12.png","/images/GlucoseToleranceTestDataAnalysis/13.png"]},{slug:"ModernRegressionAnalysis",title:"Real Estate Price Prediction Using Linear Regression Models",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/ModernRegressionAnalysis/STA9700.png",hero:"/images/ModernRegressionAnalysis/STA9700.png",pdfFile:"/STA9700.pdf",tags:["Statistics","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Regression Analysis",duration:"01/2019 - 05/2019",tools:"MATLAB",links:[]},desc:["Background","These projects focus on identifying key factors influencing home prices in Upstate New York, beyond the well-known mantra of 'location, location, location.' The dataset, sourced from publicly available records, includes features such as selling price, living area, number of bedrooms, number of fireplaces, lot size, and central air availability. The analysis applied both simple and multiple linear regression models using SPSS.","Objective","The goal was to examine how individual and combined property features predict selling price, evaluate the statistical significance of each predictor, explore potential interactions, and build the most effective regression model for price prediction.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data Setup: Random sample of home sales data with multiple numeric and categorical predictors."," \xb7 Project 1 (Simple Regression): Modeled selling price as a function of living area; evaluated model fit with R\xb2, residual diagnostics, and transformations to address normality issues."," \xb7 Project 2 (Multiple Regression): Developed models including living area, number of bedrooms, number of fireplaces, and central air with interaction terms; assessed collinearity, partial correlations, and extra sum of squares; applied backward elimination for model selection."," \xb7 Statistical Software: SPSS was used for all analysis, plotting, and diagnostics.","Results"," \xb7 Living area was consistently the strongest and most statistically significant predictor of selling price."," \xb7 In the simple regression model, a 1 sq. ft. increase in living area was associated with an estimated $116.65 increase in price (R\xb2 = 57.7%)."," \xb7 In multiple regression, the number of bedrooms had a negative coefficient when holding living area constant, suggesting smaller average room size reduces value."," \xb7 The interaction between living area and central air was significant; houses with central air gained an additional $35.61 per sq. ft. compared to those without."," \xb7 Number of fireplaces was not a statistically significant predictor.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 Living area is the dominant driver of selling price, but other features can modify its impact."," \xb7 Interaction terms can uncover nuanced relationships not visible in additive models."," \xb7 Collinearity should be monitored when combining size-related variables such as bedrooms and living area."," \xb7 Applying model diagnostics and selection techniques (e.g., backward elimination) ensures more robust and interpretable models."],gallery:[]},{slug:"CustomerChurnAnalysis",title:"Telco\u2019s Customer Churn Data Analysis",kicker:"CUNY Baruch, Information Systems and Statistics",cover:"/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/CustomerChurnAnalysis.png",hero:"/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/CustomerChurnAnalysis.png",pdfFile:"",tags:["Machine Learning","Statistics","ETL"],meta:{role:"Academic",stack:"Data Mining \xb7 Feature Selection \xb7 Feature Engineering \xb7 MODEL COMPARISON \xb7 Parameter Optimization",duration:"09/2019 - 01/2020",tools:"RapidMiner",links:[]},desc:["Background","This project focuses on predicting and understanding customer churn for a telecommunications company (Telco Ltd.) using data from January 2018. The dataset contains 7,043 customers, 20 regular attributes, and a target variable 'Churn'. The aim is both to predict churn and to identify characteristics of churned customers.","Objective"," \xb7 Task 1: Build models to predict customer churn and evaluate their performance."," \xb7 Task 2: Determine the key characteristics and segments of customers who are most likely to churn.","Methodology"," \xb7 Data Preprocessing: Data cleaning (handling missing values, converting nominal to binomial attributes), exploratory data analysis, and feature selection."," \xb7 Feature Selection: Forward selection, attribute weighting (information gain ratio, Gini index, Relief, Chi-square). Contract type and Internet Service emerged as the most important features."," \xb7 Models Compared: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Naive Bayes, Deep Learning, KNN."," \xb7 Parameter Optimization & Ensemble: Grid search, bagging, AdaBoost, voting with top models."," \xb7 Clustering: K-means clustering (K=3 and K=4) to segment churned customers."," \xb7 Tools: Python (ML models), statistical methods, clustering analysis.","Results"," \xb7 Feature Selection led to 12 key attributes; feature engineering was abandoned as it reduced performance."," \xb7 Random Forest achieved the best F-measure (78.65%) with recall 62.68%; Logistic Regression achieved recall 79.83%."," \xb7 Voting ensemble of top 3 models gave the highest performance, outperforming single models and other ensemble methods."," \xb7 Clustering (K=3) identified groups such as: (1) DSL users with mailed check, low total charges; (2) Month-to-month contract with electronic check, high monthly charge, low tenure; (3) High total charge, two-year contract."," \xb7 Clustering (K=4) provided finer segmentation, highlighting that monthly contract, low tenure, and electronic check payment are high churn risk indicators.","Key Takeaways"," \xb7 Short-term contracts, low tenure, and electronic check payments are strong churn predictors."," \xb7 High monthly charges may indicate unmet needs and risk of churn."," \xb7 Ensemble methods, particularly voting of top models, can boost prediction performance."," \xb7 Customer segmentation via clustering can guide targeted retention strategies."],gallery:["/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/1.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/2.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/9.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/3.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/4.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/5.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/6.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/7.png","/images/CustomerChurnAnalysis/8.png"]},{slug:"CPS",title:"Crime Prevention System",kicker:"City Government, New York City, NY ",cover:"/images/CPS/cover.png",hero:"/images/CPS/cover.png",tags:["C#",".NET","Angular JS","JavaScript","TypeScript","Angular","SQL","Full Stack","ETL","SSIS","System Design","Full Stack","ETL","Backend Engineer","Frontend Engineer","UI Design"],meta:{role:"Full-Stack Developer (Tech Lead)",stack:"TypeScript/Angular  \xb7  JavaScript/AngularJS  \xb7  C#/.NET ,",duration:"10/2016 \u2013 Present",tools:"Visual Studio \xb7 SQL Server Management Studio \xb7 Redis \xb7 Git \xb7 Jenkins \xb7 Octopus Deploy \xb7 IIS",links:[]},desc:["The Crime Prevention System (CPS) is a mission-critical platform supporting NYPD and DHS PD operations across 500+ NYC homeless shelters\u2014enhancing reporting efficiency, workflow transparency, and data-informed coordination at scale.",,"\u25aa\tComprehensive Reporting Suite: Designed and delivered multiple reporting modules to meet daily operational needs, including Incident Reporting, Disciplinary Records, Performance Evaluations, Training, Recruitment.",,"\u25aa\tWorkflow Management & Access Control: Implemented user- and role-based approval workflows with automated email and real-time in-app notifications. Each report module includes detailed audit logging to ensure traceability, confidentiality, and streamlined approval processes.",,"\u25aa\tInteractive Data Visualization: Delivered rich interactive features including dynamic data tables (with search, filter, sort), visualizations (bar charts, pie charts), Google Maps, KPI dashboards, and custom widgets\u2014empowering users to make data-informed decisions.",,"\u25aa\tCustom Report Export: Developed dynamic report export features allowing users to generate single-report PDFs that meet city/state government standards, as well as aggregated Excel summaries.",,"\u25aa\tSystem Architecture & Development: Designed and implemented a full-stack web application from the ground up, including front-end UI (Angular), backend RESTful APIs and services (.NET), data modeling (SQL Server), and cross-agency data integrations.",,"\u25aa\tPerformance & Scalability Optimization: Developed reusable templates and components to streamline workflows, and optimized system performance, scalability, and maintainability\u2014resulting in a more robust and efficient application.",,"\u25aa\tTeam Leadership & Full-Cycle Development: Technical lead and team player for 5 years. Led a team of 8 successfully migrating the application from .NET to .NET Core and from AngularJS to the latest Angular with TypeScript. In parallel, delivered new business modules and continuous feature enhancements. Oversaw sprint planning, task prioritization, and consistently delivered milestones on schedule.",,"\u25aa\tCross-Functional Collaboration: Coordinated efforts across ETL developers, DBAs, cloud engineers, server administrators, UI/UX teams, and the department\u2019s common service framework team to ensure smooth integration and successful project delivery."],gallery:[]},{slug:"CARES",title:"CARES",kicker:"City Government, New York City, NY",cover:"/images/CARES/cover.png",hero:"/images/CARES/cover.png",tags:["Angular","TypeScript","Frontend Engineer"],meta:{role:"Frontend Developer",stack:"Angular 6 - 8",duration:"08/2019 - 03/2020",tools:"Visual Studio \xb7 Git \xb7 Jenkins \xb7 IIS",links:[]},desc:["The CARES System is a reporting platform utilized by employees in homeless shelters to record and manage client information. ",," \u25aa\tInitiated Frontend Development: Joined the project from its inception to spearhead the frontend development. Established foundational code design and UI architecture to set the stage for a scalable and maintainable system."," \u25aa\tDeveloped Reusable Templates: Collaborated with the Business Analyst team and UI designers to create a modular design, enabling the development of code templates based on consistent UI design patterns."],gallery:[]},{slug:"SARS",title:"Security Audit & Reporting System",kicker:"Antra.Inc, Sterling, VA",cover:"/images/SARS/cover.png",hero:"/images/SARS/cover.png",tags:["C#",".NET","Angular JS","Angular","SQL","Full Stack","ETL","SSIS","JavaScript","SSRS","Full Stack","Backend Engineer","UI Design","System Design","Frontend Engineer"],meta:{role:"ETL Developer & Full-Stack Software Engineer",stack:"C#/.NET \xb7 JavaScript/Angular JS \xb7 SQL Server  \xb7 SSIS \xb7 SSRS",duration:"03/2016 - 10/2016",tools:"Visual Studio \xb7 SQL Server Management Studio \xb7 Git \xb7 IIS",links:[]},desc:["The Security Audit & Reporting System (SARS) is a comprehensive web-based application designed to facilitate audit recording and reporting. It assists auditors in reviewing controls and enhancements according to NIST Special Publication 800-53 (Rev. 4), providing supporting documentation and detailed assessments.","Engineered the web-based application using AngularJS for the frontend, RESTful APIs for backend integration, and SQL Server for data storeage, delivering a robust and scalable solution.","The high light of the project is to use the intergrted data visulization tool in a flexcible way to present chats using SQL Server Reporting Services (SSRS) to the AngularJS frontend. And the at the backend implement ETL processes using SQL Server Integration Services (SSIS) for data cleansing, transfer, and loading."],gallery:[]},{slug:"LeetCodeAchievements",title:"LeetCode Achievements",kicker:"Personal Algorithm Practice and Competitive Programming",cover:"/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 365days.png",hero:"/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 365days.png",pdfFile:"",tags:["Algorithm","Data Structures","C#"],meta:{role:"Personal",stack:"Data Structures \xb7 Algorithms \xb7 Problem Solving",duration:"2022 - Present",tools:"LeetCode Online Judge",links:[]},desc:["Background","A showcase of my progress on LeetCode, focusing on strengthening problem-solving skills and preparing for technical interviews.","Objective"," \xb7 Solve problems across all difficulty levels and topics."," \xb7 Maintain consistent practice and track improvement.","Results"," \xb7 538 problems solved: 160 Easy, 313 Medium, 65 Hard."," \xb7 Global ranking: Top 41.43%."," \xb7 365 Days Badge and 85 active days in the past year."],gallery:["/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 365days.png","/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 50days 2025.png","/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 50days 2023.png","/images/LeetCodeAchievements/LeetCode 100days 2022.png","/images/LeetCodeAchievements/Skills.png"]}],of=["Full Stack","Machine Learning","Statistics","Finance","Python","R","Frontend Engineer","Angular","Angular JS","React","JavaScript","TypeScript","Backend Engineer","C#",".NET","SQL","ETL","SSIS","SSRS","System Design","UI Design","OOD","Algorithm","Data Structures"],sf="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)",lf="#FFFF00",uf=Wt.section`
